p.src:
Squared distance is a scalar. Writing the same displacement in two coordinate systems therefore gives $g_{mn}(x)\,dx^m dx^n
 =
 g'_{pq}(y)\,dy^pdy^q.$ Use $dx^m=\frac{\partial x^m}{\partial y^p}\,dy^p$ twice: $\begin{aligned}ds^2
 &=
 g_{mn}(x)
 \frac{\partial x^m}{\partial y^p}
 \frac{\partial x^n}{\partial y^q}
 dy^pdy^q.
\end{aligned}$ Comparison with the primed expression identifies $g'_{pq}(y)
 =
 \frac{\partial x^m}{\partial y^p}
 \frac{\partial x^n}{\partial y^q}
 g_{mn}(x).$ This is exactly the transformation law for a covariant rank-two tensor.

